CloudBurrow patch 4 of 5 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1061; the bugs: #1066, #1077).

The bug: every FLOAT64 NaN read back NULL, because googlesqlite hands a
FLOAT64 to SQLite as a double, and SQLite turns a NaN into NULL both when it
is bound (value.EncodeValue, sqlite3_bind_double) and when a function returns
one (sqlitex applyResult, sqlite3_result_double): a stored NaN, a NaN query
parameter, and even SELECT IEEE_DIVIDE(0, 0), read NULL. A NaN literal was
refused too: GoogleSQL writes one as CAST("nan" AS FLOAT64), which
floatValueFromLiteral could not parse, and literalFromValue wrote a NaN or an
infinity as "NaN.0" or "+Inf.0". The patch carries a NaN past SQLite as the
value envelope googlesqlite already uses for BYTES, NUMERIC and the rest
(header "float", body "NaN"), which DecodeValue reads back as a FLOAT64 NaN
wherever a value is read; parses GoogleSQL's NaN and infinity literals; and
writes an infinity as a REAL literal too large for a double. An infinity
inside an ARRAY or a STRUCT, whose encoding is JSON (which has no infinity),
failed "json: unsupported value: +Inf" (#1077); it goes in the envelope too.
Other FLOAT64 values are bound and stored as before. nan_test.go fails without the patch
and passes with it.

